    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-17800",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250802/assets/492cbb12227ccbdd9f9c4d3e78268d41fe50db84edfa8a7a4c1cc7cd3c1f21e8.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=f(-x)-1?$",
      "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=f(-x)-1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Reflect the result across the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(-x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250802/assets/36283012eba092d1bb7f8f76ba8bc25423d5f5526e9d89ba4f69644c6d900e00.png)\n\n- Then, translate the result down by $1$ unit to get the graph of $y=f(-x)-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250802/assets/8332b0ae6cc477b451eefaf7192fcad820df3847bed535542e465f8f43994cdb.png)",
